WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:07.174
"Spices to Boost GLP-1"

00:00:07.174 --> 00:00:08.709
If you have people
eat the same meal,

00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:12.846
but chew each bite
either 15 times or 40 times,

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:13.714
the blood levels

00:00:13.714 --> 00:00:15.616
of the appetite-suppressing
hormone GLP-1

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:16.316
in the blood stream

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:19.453
are higher
after 40 chews than 15,

00:00:19.453 --> 00:00:20.988
and those who chewed 40 times

00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:23.657
ended up consuming
about 75 fewer calories

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:25.826
than the 15-time chewers.

00:00:25.826 --> 00:00:27.828
The researchers suggest
that chewing more

00:00:27.828 --> 00:00:30.330
may help people
keep their weight down.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.965
Okay, but who wants to sit there

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.034
and count how many times
they chew?

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:37.170
What if you just eat
chewier foods?

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.139
What if you have people
eat shredded cabbage,

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.674
which requires a lot of chewing,

00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:42.910
or the same amount
of pureed cabbage;

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.446
same food, but requiring
different amounts of chewing?

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:48.482
GLP-1 blood levels were higher

00:00:48.482 --> 00:00:50.517
in the shredded cabbage
chewing group

00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:53.453
than the pureed cabbage
non-chewing one,

00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:56.723
at 45, 60, and 90 minutes,
at least initially.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.592
The researchers were careful
to make sure

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.861
both groups ate
at the same rate,

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:03.463
because eating
even the same food more slowly

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:06.400
can result in a greater
GLP-1 response.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.901
Researchers had people

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.370
eat the same amount of ice cream

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.839
over a period of either
five minutes or 30 minutes,

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.574
and those who ate
the exact same amount

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.343
of the same food more slowly

00:01:16.343 --> 00:01:19.146
experienced a significant boost
in GLP-1 levels in their blood

00:01:19.146 --> 00:01:20.747
for hours after the meal,

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:23.850
about a 30 percent bump overall.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.653
On average, the participants
were overweight,

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.456
but a lot slimmer
than your average American.

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:31.725
What about essentially
re-doing the same study,

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:33.393
but with obese individuals

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:36.096
who could really use
the extra GLP-1 boost?

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:38.332
And here we go.

00:01:38.332 --> 00:01:40.133
As you can see from the title,

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.069
the researchers found
disparate results

00:01:42.069 --> 00:01:44.104
when they tested
obese adolescents

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:45.939
versus obese adults.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.742
With the obese teens,
researchers saw the same outcome

00:01:48.742 --> 00:01:50.210
as they did with
the overweight adults;

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.746
a significantly higher
GLP-1 response

00:01:52.746 --> 00:01:54.348
after slower eating.

00:01:54.348 --> 00:01:55.349
But the rate of eating

00:01:55.349 --> 00:01:58.085
didn’t seem to matter
in obese adults.

00:01:58.085 --> 00:01:59.186
Same thing with satiety.

00:01:59.186 --> 00:02:00.988
Obese adolescents felt fuller,

00:02:00.988 --> 00:02:03.924
more satiated for longer
when eating more slowly,

00:02:03.924 --> 00:02:05.792
but speed of eating
didn’t seem to matter

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.261
in the adults with obesity.

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.363
But, at least for some,

00:02:09.363 --> 00:02:11.198
more chewing
and eating more slowly

00:02:11.198 --> 00:02:14.468
may raise GLP-1 levels,
no matter what’s consumed.

00:02:14.468 --> 00:02:17.971
But are there certain foods
that specifically boost GLP-1?

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:19.740
In my last video,
I went through a bunch of foods,

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.376
beverages, and supplements
that don’t appear to work,

00:02:22.376 --> 00:02:25.879
but there are
a few spices that might.

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:28.982
Volunteers were served
rice with vegetable curry

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:31.451
made with three different
doses of spices.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:34.287
The bland control meal
had no spices at all,

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:36.556
just tomato puree with eggplant.

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:39.860
The low-spice meal added
a tablespoon of curry spices,

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.528
plus onions, garlic, and ginger.

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:44.031
And the third variation,
the high-spice curry,

00:02:44.031 --> 00:02:46.299
had double the spices,
two tablespoons.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:49.936
The spices were turmeric,
coriander seeds, cumin seeds,

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.539
dried Indian gooseberry powder--
also known as amla--

00:02:52.539 --> 00:02:54.741
cayenne pepper,
cinnamon, and cloves

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:57.377
mixed in the ratio of eight
to four to four to four,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:03:00.347
to two to one to one,
respectively.

00:03:00.347 --> 00:03:02.949
And the average bump
in GLP-1 blood concentration

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:04.951
for those eating
both the low-spice

00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:05.852
and high-spice meals

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.722
were 17 and 32 percent higher,

00:03:08.722 --> 00:03:11.758
compared to the bland
control meal without the spices.

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:13.593
About the same calories
and macronutrients,

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:16.863
but the spicier meals
raised GLP-1 levels higher.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:19.800
But which spice was it?

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.337
Ginger compounds boosts
GLP-1 in mice but not rats,

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:27.374
and in humans, we appeared
to react more like the rats:

00:03:27.374 --> 00:03:28.475
no effect.

00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:31.545
For many of the other spices,
there isn’t even in vitro data,

00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:33.346
but rather only in silico,

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:35.315
meaning just some kind
of computer modeling

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:38.552
that didn’t actually test
anything in a biological system.

00:03:38.552 --> 00:03:40.287
But we do have data on curcumin,

00:03:40.287 --> 00:03:43.256
the yellow compound
found in the spice turmeric.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.826
This study used
180 milligrams of curcumin,

00:03:45.826 --> 00:03:49.162
which is the amount found in
a single teaspoon of turmeric.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.598
The researchers also tested
fish oil supplements,

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:54.067
but while the fish oil failed,
the curcumin

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.103
reduced the blood sugar spike
after a meal,

00:03:56.103 --> 00:04:00.340
which is something both
GLP-1 and GLP-1 drugs can do,

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.210
though GLP-1 levels
weren’t directly measured.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.679
The researchers suggest
it may be a GLP-1 effect,

00:04:05.679 --> 00:04:08.348
since curcumin
stimulates GLP-1 in rodents,

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:10.283
as well as cells
in a Petri dish,

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:14.988
but there hasn’t been
a human study until now.

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:16.690
Six months
of curcumin supplementation

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.825
led to a quadrupling
of GLP-1 levels,

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.594
compared to placebo.

00:04:20.594 --> 00:04:22.729
Okay, so, turmeric may help.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:24.865
What about cinnamon?

00:04:24.865 --> 00:04:27.834
Scandinavian researchers
gave people rice pudding

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:31.004
with and without
one or three grams of cinnamon,

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:32.239
which is about
a third of a teaspoon,

00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:33.707
or a full teaspoon.

00:04:33.707 --> 00:04:35.675
And those getting
the full teaspoon of cinnamon

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:38.245
more than doubled
the GLP-1 bump-from-baseline

00:04:38.245 --> 00:04:41.148
compared to the control pudding
without the cinnamon.

00:04:41.148 --> 00:04:43.416
So, that’s another spice
that may work,

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.253
though people didn’t report
feeling any more satiated

00:04:46.253 --> 00:04:49.489
eating the cinnamon-y pudding
compared to plain.

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:53.059
And finally, the third spice
to boost GLP-1 in humans?

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:54.761
Cayenne pepper.

00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:56.296
What happened
when a half a teaspoon

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.198
of cayenne pepper
was added to a meal?

00:04:58.198 --> 00:05:01.334
Over a short period of time--
15 minutes--

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.470
a single spicy meal
significantly increased

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:05.605
blood levels of GLP-1.

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:06.773
Though,
like in the cinnamon study,

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:10.377
this did not appear to translate
into them feeling any fuller,

00:05:10.377 --> 00:05:11.811
but the researcher
didn’t measure

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:13.814
actual subsequent food intake.

